<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ananda Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ananda Temple is a Buddhist temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagan">Bagan</a> in Myanmar, completed around 1105 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a> of the Pagan Kingdom. Built on a cross-shaped plan with four standing Buddha images facing the cardinal points and hundreds of glazed terracotta plaques illustrating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a>, it is one of the largest and best preserved temples of Bagan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ananda-temple.jpg" alt="Ananda Temple" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagan,_Myanmar,_Ananda_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple takes its name from Ananda, the first cousin, attendant and one of the principal disciples of the Buddha; the word means bliss in Pali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. A legend holds that eight monks who had meditated in the Nandamula Cave in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> described it to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a>, who asked them to build a temple like it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagan">Bagan</a> plain, and then had them killed so that they could build no other. The scholar George Coedes recorded a different story, in which the architect and a child were buried alive to serve as guardian spirits.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a> had adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> and used the temple to present its teachings to his people in stone images, numbered plaques and standing Buddhas. Its building is taken to mark the end of the Early Bagan period and the start of the Middle period. The archaeologist Duroiselle held that its architects were Indians, and its design is often compared with temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> and with the Ananta cave temple in the Udayagiri hills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Built of brick and plaster, the temple has a central square of 53 metres, with a gabled porch projecting from each face, and rises 51 metres through two curved roofs and four terraces to a small pagoda crowned by a hti umbrella. Its core is a cube with a Buddha 9.5 metres tall on each face: Kassapa to the south, Kakusandha to the north, Konagamana to the east and Gautama to the west. The northern and southern images are originals; the other two replaced images lost to fire.</p><p>Two vaulted passages run round the cube, lined with 80 reliefs of the Buddha's life carved from volcanic rock, and about 1500 stone images survive inside. At the feet of the western Buddha kneel lacquer figures of the king and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shin-arahan">Shin Arahan</a>, the Mon monk who converted him. Glazed terracotta plaques on the base and terraces show the army of Mara and the jataka tales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The temple was damaged in the earthquake of 1975 and has since been restored, with help from the Indian government. Its spires were gilded in 1990 for the 900th anniversary of its building. A brick monastery of 1137, the Ananda Okkyaung, stands within its precincts. Each year in the month of Pyatho, from December to January, a week-long festival is held there, when 1,000 monks chant the scriptures for 72 hours and villagers camp around the temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ananda Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kyansittha, who ruled from 1084 to c. 1113, consolidated the state, completed the Shwezigon stupa and built the <b>Ananda</b> temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
